  "transcript": "# The UK’s constitutional safeguards\n\n## Two different threats\n> Constitutional risk comes from both abuse of power and government failure to deliver; both can undermine legitimacy.\n\nChair: Do you think the UK’s constitutional democracy is under threat, and what did you see in No. 10?\nMorgan McSweeney: There are always pressures. Two ways a constitution is threatened: abuse of power, and Governments failing to deliver their lawful programmes. Populists exploit the latter. Both can erode legitimacy.\nLord Murphy of Torfaen: Would you want changes to the UK constitution?\nMorgan McSweeney: Yes. There are many rules shaping Prime Ministerial power. Individually they often made sense, but together they form a thicket. We should identify fewer, justified rules and consider codifying the essentials—like the Cabinet Manual—so roles are clearer.\n\n\n## Who should decide changes — and why clarity matters\n> Reform should be driven by Parliament, not the Prime Minister, and clarity stops inconsistent interpretations that slow government.\n\nLord Murphy of Torfaen: Could the Ministerial Code be made statutory?\nMorgan McSweeney: Possibly. But first do a wider review of accumulated rules. Many were added after mistakes; they now work by precedent. Parliament should lead any codification so it’s not just the Prime Minister deciding.\nLord Beith: What if a Prime Minister says delivery is urgent and overrides other safeguards?\nMorgan McSweeney: There are safeguards—courts, Parliament, Cabinet, civil servants, parties, elections. They should remain. But we need clarity so Prime Ministers know the limits of their power and won’t rough-guess based on different civil‑service interpretations.\nLord Griffiths of Burry Port: How do you ensure leaders have the character and judgment to respect conventions?\nMorgan McSweeney: Codes can't guarantee character. Parties play a role—choosing candidates and leaders—and voters ultimately judge. We can only make the rules clear and robust enough to constrain abuse.\n\n\n## Quasi-judicial decisions and Secretary of State autonomy\n> Some laws interpreted to mean a single Secretary of State must decide can stop necessary Cabinet discussion; fix interpretation, not always the law.\n\nBaroness Hamwee: You mentioned judicial review earlier; do you think courts intervene too much?\nMorgan McSweeney: I misspoke. My concern is 'quasi-judicial processes'—laws that say 'the Secretary of State shall decide' and which are interpreted to prevent Cabinet-level consultation. That interpretation can block timely, cross-department discussion.\nLord Jones of Penybont: So what do you want changed—the law or its practice?\nMorgan McSweeney: Often the law's application or precedent causes the problem. I wouldn't remove Secretary of State decision-making where statute requires it. But where decisions affect many departments, there should be a clear route for Cabinet or PM input while preserving the Secretary of State's formal role.\nLord Beith: Is the autonomy of Secretaries of State part of the constitution or just practice?\nMorgan McSweeney: Where statute names the Secretary of State, that should stand. The reform needed is clearer rules about consultation and when collective discussion is appropriate, especially for national-security or economy-wide matters.\n\n\n## Civil Service role and the centre of government\n> The Civil Service should protect the constitution and advise on legality; the centre needs more strategic cohesion and coordination.\n\nLord Bichard: Should civil servants act to protect the constitution beyond advising Ministers?\nMorgan McSweeney: They should and largely do. But distinguish two cases: if a civil servant thinks a Minister is acting unlawfully, different routes are needed than when a civil servant thinks a Minister is ignoring convention.\nLord Bichard: So civil servants might resist implementing political wishes to defend constitutional principles?\nMorgan McSweeney: Yes, if the action is unlawful. When it’s a matter of changing precedent or convention, civil servants must implement lawful decisions—even if they disagree—while flagging risks and using informal channels to resolve disputes first.\nLord Waldegrave of North Hill: You said the centre of government is 'too weak'—how would you strengthen it?\nMorgan McSweeney: Size isn’t the same as strength. The centre needs coherent coordination: strategic capacity that plans ahead for things like Budgets and King's Speeches, and better coordination of communications and cross-department delivery, not more fragmented teams.\nLord Waldegrave of North Hill: So more central strategy and joined-up teams, not just more staff?\nMorgan McSweeney: Exactly. Clear priorities from the Prime Minister, routed through a stronger centre that can chase departments and produce consistent action.\n\n\n## Parliament, parties and practical checks\n> Parliament, parties and political reality are essential checks; reforms should come from Parliament and MPs remain capable of holding government to account.\n\nBaroness Hamwee: Can the Commons act as a restraint when interpretations go against Parliament’s intent?\nMorgan McSweeney: Yes. Parliament can amend bad legislation, use PMQs, committees, or pass new laws. It should be central to deciding which rules stay, which change, and to any codification.\nLord Cryer: But haven't both Cabinet and Commons become weaker over decades?\nMorgan McSweeney: My experience is MPs are well informed and engaged on national issues. Cabinets still include strong figures who will push back. No single check is perfect—courts, Parliament, Cabinet, civil servants and parties all matter together.\nLord Beith: Who should lead the review of accumulated rules?\nMorgan McSweeney: Parliament should lead it—committees like this can recommend. The Prime Minister shouldn’t alone rewrite conventions; that risks politicising rules that protect the constitution.\nChair: Any final practical example of change you’d press for?\nMorgan McSweeney: Review quasi‑judicial interpretations to allow necessary Cabinet input; codify core roles in the Cabinet Manual so everyone—Ministers, civil servants and the public—knows who decides what and when.\nChair: Thank you for your time and for joining us from Chicago this morning.\n",
